  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nThe blue letter H will stay on the weather maps throughout the \nforecast period as atmospheric pressure will be as high as 1034 \nmillibars. Thermodynamically, the days will be mild with high \ntemperatures in the 60s. The exception comes around midweek, \nTuesday into Wednesday, when temperatures only climb into the \n50s after a cold front passage late Monday. At night, \nradiational cooling will take place under clear skies, so lows \nwill be in the 20s and 30s through the weekend and into early \nnext week. The return of southerly flow later in the week will \nkeep the overnight periods more mild. Patchy fog will also be \npossible each night.\n\nHydrologically, precipitation chances will be minimal for another \nweek. The aforementioned cold front on Monday will dry out as it \nmoves into the region though some models are hinting at some \nsprinkles or even light rain for the Tug Hill and Mohawk Valley. \nThere will be quite a bit of dry air to overcome though, so \nPoPs are not high enough yet to mention in the forecast. Rain \nchances may be better late in the week but even then, it does \nnot look like a guarantee. A warm front/cold front combo will \npass through. Some models have showers with the warm front while\nothers do not. Then with the cold front, it runs into the drier\nair and showers fall apart, similar to the front earlier in the\nweek.",
